00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.110 align:start position:0%
russia's defense ministry says these are  some of its troops which had been  positioned on the border with ukraine  they're returning to base after  finishing training exercises  as the training comes to an end the  troops as usual will march back to their  home stations units from the southern  and western military districts that  completed their tasks already have  started loading on trains and motor  vehicles they will start moving to their  home bases individual units will march  on their own in military columns  while this raises hopes that tensions  could ease between russia and ukraine  there are still large-scale military  exercises taking place  and it's not clear how many units are  being pulled back  news of the troops drawdown came just as  the german chancellor olaf schultz  arrived in russia for talks with  president vladimir putin who said war  was the last thing he wanted  do we want war or not of course we don't  and it's for that reason that we have  submitted a proposal as to a negotiation  process as a result of which there  should be a guarantee for security for  everybody including our country  chancellor olaf schultz said some  aspects of russia's demands on ukraine  were worth considering while stressing  the need for diplomacy to succeed the  diplomatism  today i fully agree that diplomatic  efforts have yet to be exhausted  we have to find a peaceful resolution to  this crisis  several troops have been pulled back  which is a good sign and we hope that  this will continue in the belgian  capital nato secretary general jen  stoltenberg expressed cautious optimism  at the news of the russian drawdown  there are signs from moscow that  diplomacy should continue  this gives  grounds for cautious optimism  but so far  we have not seen any sign of  de-escalation on the ground despite  these intense diplomatic efforts there  are still many outstanding issues at the  heart of the crisis  vladimir putin says he is ready to talk  to u.s leaders about ukraine's request  to join the 30-member nato alliance and  he says that is a conversation which  cannot be put off any longer something  both nato and u.s leaders have refused  to discuss so far  and until that issue is resolved it is  now clear that this crisis could  continue for months to come  dorsal jabari al jazeera moscow
 

